          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "Yes. Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for long-term insomnia, and it works without medication. The insomnia guideline in this collection describes it as a structured programme with several parts. These include limiting time in bed to match actual sleep time, then gradually extending it as sleep improves. Other parts are changing beliefs that keep you awake, such as worrying about tomorrow, and relaxation training. (Source: *Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*.) The guideline notes that digitally delivered and brief formats are reasonable if in-person therapy is hard to access.\n\nDaily habits support sleep but do not replace CBT-I. The most useful habit described is a consistent wake time, held seven days a week, because your body clock responds most strongly to morning light. Bedtime can vary more. (Source: *Sleep habits for insomnia*.) The guideline is explicit that sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment for chronic insomnia.\n\nSeek professional review if you notice any of these, because they need assessment beyond insomnia treatment (Source: *Insomnia*):\n- loud snoring with pauses in breathing that someone else has seen\n- excessive daytime sleepiness, or falling asleep while driving\n- acting out dreams\n- sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness\n- headaches that wake you or are present on most mornings (Source: *Sleep and migraine*)\n\nIf you feel hopeless or have thoughts of harming yourself, contact a doctor or crisis service now rather than waiting for a routine appointment.\n\nThe sources I searched do not cover how to find a CBT-I provider, how long the programme takes, or what it costs. A GP or sleep clinic can explain those options.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
